/// Chapter 30 of the guide, written from md-docs/30-security.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page SecurityGuide(path: "/docs/guide/security", title: "Security", description: "What WebFluent enforces, and how to handle outside markup, sessions, env secrets, uploads, CSP headers, other origins and a deploy checklist.", layout: DocsShell("Security", chapter: t("nav.group.quality"), blurb: "What the compiler guarantees, what it cannot, and the decisions left to you: markup, secrets, storage, headers and a deployment checklist.", source: "md-docs/30-security.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "what-the-compiler-guarantees", label: "What the compiler guarantees"), Anchor(id: "what-it-cannot-guarantee", label: "What it cannot guarantee"), Anchor(id: "show-markup-you-did-not-write", label: "Show markup you did not write"), Anchor(id: "keep-the-session-in-a-cookie", label: "Keep the session in a cookie"), Anchor(id: "keep-secrets-out-of-the-bundle", label: "Keep secrets out of the bundle"), Anchor(id: "check-uploads-on-the-server", label: "Check uploads on the server"), Anchor(id: "ship-a-content-security-policy", label: "Ship a Content-Security-Policy"), Anchor(id: "pin-another-origin-s-files", label: "Pin another origin's files"), Anchor(id: "sanitize-what-your-own-scripts-insert", label: "Sanitize what your own scripts insert"), Anchor(id: "audit-the-project", label: "Audit the project"), Anchor(id: "before-you-deploy", label: "Before you deploy")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/testing", prevLabel: t("ch.29"), nextTo: "/docs/guide/performance", nextLabel: t("ch.31"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter shows you how to keep a WebFluent site safe: what the compiler already stops, what it cannot stop, and the decisions it leaves to you.\n\nA web page is a program you hand to a stranger's machine, and its strings come from everywhere: a form, a URL, a CMS, a database, another service. Most security bugs are one mistake — a string that arrives somewhere it becomes *code* instead of *content*. The compiler closes most of those doors for you. The rest are listed here, with what to do about each.\n\nIn this chapter:\n\n- what the compiler guarantees, and what it cannot;\n- how to show markup from outside, keep a session, keep secrets out of the bundle and handle uploads;\n- how to ship a Content-Security-Policy and trust other origins' files;\n- how to audit a project, and a checklist for deploying.")

        Heading("What the compiler guarantees", id: "what-the-compiler-guarantees").h2

        Markdown("These are properties of the output, not advice. You do not have to turn them on.\n\n- **Text is text.** Every string an element shows goes in as a text node. `\"<script>\"` in your data can never become a script. The one exception has `Unsafe` in its name ([Show markup you did not write](#show-markup-you-did-not-write)). An `html:` argument on an element is an attribute called `html`, as any argument an element does not declare is (`C04`), and never markup.\n- **No inline handlers.** Events are bound with `addEventListener`. `Button(\"x\", onclick: expr)` is a compile error, because an `on*` attribute is the one place a browser runs an attribute's value. Write `on click \{ \}` instead. `style:` is refused the same way (`E106`): a value from data would be the element's whole inline style. A `style \{ \}` block sets one property at a time.\n- **An attribute holds its value.** A `class:` from data, a page title from a route parameter or front matter, a chart's or a QR code's colour: each is escaped for where it is written, and a colour must be a colour (`#0F766E`, `red`, `rgb(…)`, `var(--x)`) or the default is drawn.\n- **No URL a browser would run.** `href:`, `src:`, `to:`, `poster:` and `navigate()` take a URL whose scheme is `http`, `https`, `mailto`, `tel`, `sms` or `ftp`, or a URL with no scheme at all. The compiler refuses a literal where you write it, and the runtime refuses a value that exists only at run time at the moment it is used. A page may never point at `javascript:`, `data:`, `vbscript:`, `blob:` or `file:`.\n- **Except a picture in its address.** A `data:image/png;base64,…` or `data:image/svg+xml,…` URL is allowed where a picture is drawn: an `Image`'s or an `Avatar`'s `src:`, a Markdown picture, and an `<img>` that `sanitize` keeps. An `<img>` never runs what it shows, not even an SVG's scripts. A link, a frame or a form never takes one, and `data:text/html` is not a picture.\n- **No handle on your page.** A link with `target:` carries `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"`, so the page it opens cannot navigate yours; `sanitize` adds both to such a link whatever its `rel` said, and keeps `opener` on none.\n- **`navigate()` stays on the site.** A value the page was handed — `navigate(query.next ?? \"/\")` after a sign-in — goes to a page of the site; an address on another origin (`//elsewhere.example`) is refused, and the reader stays where they are. Leave the site with a `Link`.\n- **A frame drawing HTML from a value runs none of it.** A `Frame(srcdoc: value)` with no `sandbox:` is given `sandbox=\"\"`, and the build names it (`V03`). Its `on message` hears only what that frame's own document posts, from the origin that document has, not a page it navigated to.\n- **A page's `head \{ \}` points nowhere a browser runs.** Its `src:` and `href:` go through the scheme check at build time and in the browser. A `script(src:)` must be written out, and a refresh may not take its address from a value (`E107`): either would let whoever controls the value choose what the page runs or where the reader goes.\n- **Markdown is escaped first.** `Markdown(text)` shows any HTML in the text instead of running it. Its links and pictures go through the same scheme check as every other URL: a link to a `javascript:` URL is drawn as its words, with no link, at build time and in the browser alike. So is one that puts a backslash before its colon (`javascript\\:`), which Markdown reads as the colon it escapes. For text somebody else wrote, such as a language server's documentation, `Markdown(text).inert` draws no link or picture at all ([Content](/docs/guide/content#show-text-somebody-else-wrote-with-inert)).\n- **A `Secret` cannot escape.** It may not be shown, spliced into a string, logged or persisted. Each is a `T12`, reported at the line that does it.\n- **A non-public `env` name cannot be read from a page.** See [Keep secrets out of the bundle](#keep-secrets-out-of-the-bundle).")

        Heading("What it cannot guarantee", id: "what-it-cannot-guarantee").h2

        Markdown("Some decisions are outside the compiler's reach:\n\n- **What your server does with what you send it.** Injection into a SQL query, a template or a shell happens on the other side of the wire.\n- **Whether a reader may see a thing.** `guard:` on a page hides a route; it does not authorise anything. Authorisation is the server's answer, and the server checks it on every request.\n- **A dependency you add yourself.** A `<script>` in `public/`, a font from another origin, an embed — each is code you choose to trust.\n- **Your hosting.** HTTPS, HSTS, cookie flags and rate limits are the host's job.")

        Heading("Show markup you did not write", id: "show-markup-you-did-not-write").h2

        Markdown("Sometimes the content really is HTML: a CMS body, the output of a Markdown renderer of your own, an email. To show it, pass it through `sanitize` and into `Unsafe.Html`, the one door for markup.\n\nThis page fetches a post and shows its HTML body:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Post { title: String, body: String }

api Backend(base: "/api") {
    get post(slug: String) at "posts/:slug" -> Post
}

page PostPage(path: "/p/:slug", title: "Post", description: "One post.", slug: String) {
    resource post = Backend.post(slug: slug)
    match post {
        ready(p) {
            Heading(p.title).h1
            Unsafe.Html(sanitize(p.body))
        }
        else { Skeleton }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The two calls do different jobs:\n\n- `Unsafe.Html(markup)` puts markup in as markup. Its name lets a review find every one with a search for `Unsafe`, and the build prints a `V03` at each, saying whether the markup went through `sanitize`.\n- `sanitize(html)` is an **allow-list**: a fixed set of elements and, for each element, a fixed set of attributes. A tag nobody has thought of is dropped, not permitted. It keeps no `<script>`, `<style>`, `<iframe>` or `<form>`, and no `on*` attribute, and every `href` and `src` goes through the same scheme check as every other URL. A dropped element keeps its text, so removing a `<font>` does not remove the sentence inside it.\n\n`sanitize` runs at build time as well as in the browser, from the same allow-list. A pre-rendered page therefore shows the sanitised body at once, not an empty box that fills in when the script loads.")

        Callout("Use `Unsafe.Html(raw)` without `sanitize` only for markup **your project produced**. Anything that arrived from outside goes through `sanitize`.", kind: .caution)

        Heading("Keep the session in a cookie", id: "keep-the-session-in-a-cookie").h2

        Markdown("`persist` writes to `localStorage` or `sessionStorage`. **Neither is a place for a session token.** Any script that reaches your page can read both, and that is the whole payload of a cross-site scripting bug: without a readable token the bug is a defacement; with one it is an account takeover.\n\nTo keep a session safe, have the server set it as an **httpOnly cookie**:")

        CodeBlock(#"Set-Cookie: session=…; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Path=/; Max-Age=1209600"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("Each flag closes one door:\n\n- `HttpOnly` puts the cookie out of reach of every script, including yours.\n- `SameSite=Lax` stops another site's form from riding on it.\n- `Secure` keeps it off plaintext connections.\n\nThe browser sends the cookie with each request, so the page needs no token at all. An `api` with `credentials: .sameOrigin` sends it with every call:")

        CodeBlock(#"type User { id: String, name: String }

api Backend(base: "/api/v1") {
    credentials: .sameOrigin
    get me() -> User
    post signIn(body: Map) -> User
    post signOut()
}

store Session {
    state user: User? = null
    action load() { user = await Backend.me() }
    action out() { await Backend.signOut()  user = null  Backend.me.invalidate()  navigate("/") }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`Backend.me.invalidate()` on sign-out drops what the endpoint cached, so the next reader of the tab is not shown the last one's; do the same for every endpoint whose answer is one reader's. An answer cached under one `Authorization` header is never served for another, but a cookie cannot be read, so the cache cannot tell one cookie's reader from the next.\n\nA page *may* keep what a page may show: a display name, a theme, a preference. If a value would matter to someone who stole it, it belongs behind a request.\n\nThe compiler enforces the part it can. Declare a token as a `Secret`, and keeping it with `persist` is a `T12`:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Account(path: "/", title: "Account", description: "Your account.") {
    persist _token: Secret = ""
    Heading("Account").h1
}"#, title: "wf · draws T12")

        Heading("Keep secrets out of the bundle", id: "keep-secrets-out-of-the-bundle").h2

        Markdown("`env.NAME` is replaced by its value **at build time**, and the value lands in the JavaScript the browser downloads. That is right for an API's base address and wrong for an API key. So a page may read only a name that is public: one that begins `PUBLIC_`, or one that `public_env` in the config lists.")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "env": { "PUBLIC_API": "/api/v1", "ANALYTICS_ID": "UA-1", "STRIPE_SECRET": "sk_…" },
  "public_env": ["ANALYTICS_ID"]
}"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("With this config:\n\n- a page may read `env.PUBLIC_API` and `env.ANALYTICS_ID`;\n- `env.STRIPE_SECRET`, read from a page, a component, a store or an `api` block, is an `E108` compile error naming the file and the line.\n\nThe value of `STRIPE_SECRET` is still there for `wf render`, which runs on a server where a secret stays secret ([Server rendering](/docs/guide/server-rendering)). A secret that has to reach a third party belongs in a request your server makes, not in a page. [Environments](/docs/guide/environments) covers where `env` values come from.")

        Heading("Check uploads on the server", id: "check-uploads-on-the-server").h2

        Markdown("A `File` from `FileUpload` is a handle on a file on the reader's machine. `accept:` and `.size` help the reader pick the right file. They are **not** checks: both live in the page, and a page is the attacker's to rewrite.\n\nThe server decides what it accepts. Have it:\n\n- read the type from the file's bytes, not its name;\n- enforce a size limit itself;\n- generate the stored file's name, never use the one it was given;\n- store uploads somewhere nothing is served from.")

        Heading("Ship a Content-Security-Policy", id: "ship-a-content-security-policy").h2

        Markdown("A Content-Security-Policy (CSP) tells the browser which scripts, styles, images and connections a page may use. To ship one, set `\"build\": \{ \"csp\": true \}`. A project `wf init` creates has it on. The build then writes:\n\n- a `<meta http-equiv=\"Content-Security-Policy\">` in every page, right after `<meta charset>`: a meta policy governs only what the browser parses after it, so it stands before the title, the sharing tags and a page's own `head \{ \}` tags, which a page's data reaches;\n- a `_headers` file for hosts that read one (Netlify, Cloudflare Pages, Vercel; other hosts ignore it).\n\n`frame-ancestors` is only in `_headers`, because a browser ignores it in a meta tag. A policy that carried it there would say one thing and do another.\n\nThe policy is `'self'` throughout, with no `'unsafe-inline'` for scripts: the compiler writes external files and binds events with `addEventListener`, so nothing needs it. The build widens it by exactly the origins that `meta.fonts`, `meta.stylesheets` and `meta.scripts` declare, so the policy never blocks a font or library you declared. Your own scripts under `src/` are served from the site itself and need nothing added.")

        Heading("Keep other sites from framing your pages", id: "keep-other-sites-from-framing-your-pages").h3

        Markdown("By default no other site may frame your pages. `_headers` says `frame-ancestors 'none'` and `X-Frame-Options: DENY`, and `wf serve` sends the same, so nobody can lay your page under theirs to trick a reader into clicking it.\n\nA project that frames a page of its own — a `Frame` whose `src:` is site-relative, such as `Frame(src: \"/preview/\", title: \"Preview\")` — is relaxed to its own origin and no further: `frame-ancestors 'self'` and `X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN`. A `Frame` of another origin's page changes nothing here; that is `meta.frame`'s `frame-src`, below.")

        Heading("Allow requests, images and frames from other origins", id: "allow-requests-images-and-frames-from-other-origins").h3

        Markdown("A page may send requests only to its own origin. To allow another — an API on another domain, an analytics endpoint — name it in `meta.connect`. Each entry is an origin, such as `https://api.example.com`, or `https://*.example.com` for its subdomains, and it joins `connect-src`. An entry with a path, or a keyword such as `'self'`, is `E111`.\n\nImages follow the same rule: a page may load them from its own origin and `data:`, unless `meta.img` names others, which join `img-src`. Name a CDN there, or the pixels a tag requests (`https://www.googletagmanager.com/a`, `/td`), which a tag assistant otherwise reports as blocked.\n\nFrames are the same again: `meta.frame` names the origins a page may embed — a video player (`https://www.youtube-nocookie.com`), a map — and they join `frame-src`.\n\nThis config loads Google's tag manager after the page and allows what it sends and draws:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "meta": {
    "scripts": [{ "src": "https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=G-XXXXXXX", "load": "after" }],
    "connect": ["https://*.google-analytics.com", "https://*.analytics.google.com",
                "https://www.googletagmanager.com"],
    "img": ["https://*.google-analytics.com", "https://www.googletagmanager.com"]
  }
}"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("A tag manager's own set-up — the `dataLayer` and the `gtag('config', …)` call a vendor gives you as an inline `<script>` — goes in a plain script under `src/`, which the policy already allows.")

        Heading("Allow an app's own schemes, WebAssembly and style elements", id: "allow-an-app-s-own-schemes-webassembly-and-style-elements").h3

        Markdown("Three settings widen the policy for pages that need more:\n\n- **An app's own scheme.** An app that serves its pages itself — a desktop shell — has schemes of its own. `meta.connect` and `meta.frame` take one as a scheme alone: `\"ipc:\"` for the channel a shell's pages call it on, `\"wfpreview:\"` for a frame the app fills from its own protocol. A scheme named in `meta.frame` is also one a frame's `src` may use: the address is kept, where any other unknown scheme is emptied. The web's schemes are refused: `https:` alone would allow every origin, and `data:`, `blob:` and `javascript:` run whatever a page writes.\n- **WebAssembly.** A page that compiles WebAssembly — a parser, an image codec — sets `meta.wasm: true`, and `script-src` gains `'wasm-unsafe-eval'`. WebAssembly compiles; `eval` is still refused.\n- **Style elements.** A page whose scripts write `<style>` elements — an editor such as CodeMirror, a chart library — sets `meta.style_elements: true`, and `style-src` gains `'unsafe-inline'`, for styles only.")

        Heading("Let the build check its own output", id: "let-the-build-check-its-own-output").h3

        Markdown("**The build holds every page to the policy it ships.** It reads each HTML file back and stops on anything the policy would block: an inline `<script>`, a `<style>`, a `style=` attribute, an `on*` attribute, a script or stylesheet from an origin the policy never named or from an address that names no place (`data:`, `javascript:`), a `<base>`, or a refresh that sends the reader to another site. It reads the HTML as a browser does — names in any case, attributes apart by white space or a `/`, values quoted or not. A policy is a promise about what a page contains; without this check, the browser would enforce it and show a blank page. A page the check refuses is removed from the output, so a deploy that ignores the failed build does not publish it.\n\nA page's own `head \{ script(src: …) \}` or stylesheet `link` is added on the live page, after the HTML is written, so the compiler checks it where you write it instead: one from an origin the config does not declare is an `E902` on that line.\n\nOne thing is allowed rather than refused: a `style \{ \}` value that **reads state** is written on the element, because it has nowhere else to go. When a build emits one, `style-src` gains `'unsafe-inline'` and the build names the pages that caused it, so the stricter policy is one edit away. A literal in a `style \{ \}` block is already a shared class and costs nothing.")

        Heading("Pin another origin's files", id: "pin-another-origin-s-files").h2

        Markdown("A stylesheet or a font from another origin is code that origin can change after you read it. To pin it, declare its hash in `meta.integrity`:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "meta": {
    "stylesheets": ["https://cdn.example.com/brand.css"],
    "integrity": { "https://cdn.example.com/brand.css": "sha384-…" }
  }
}"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("The build emits `integrity` and `crossorigin=\"anonymous\"` on the tag, and warns (`D05`) about a declared external asset that has no hash. It never fetches the file to work the hash out, because that would make a build depend on the network. A Google Fonts stylesheet is exempt: Google writes it per browser, so its bytes differ between readers and no hash can match.")

        Heading("Sanitize what your own scripts insert", id: "sanitize-what-your-own-scripts-insert").h2

        Markdown("A `.js` file under `src/` runs on every page exactly as written. The compiler reads its names; it does not rewrite or sandbox it. It is code you trust, like the rest of `src/`. What it puts in the page with `innerHTML` is markup, so markup from outside the project goes through `WF.sanitize(…)` there too.\n\nA library from another origin belongs in `meta.scripts`, with an integrity hash, so a change at that origin cannot reach your readers ([JavaScript interop](/docs/guide/interop)).")

        Heading("Audit the project", id: "audit-the-project").h2

        Markdown("To answer the questions a security review asks, run:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf audit")
            ])

        Markdown("It lists:\n\n- every `Unsafe.*`, and whether it is sanitised;\n- the project's own scripts, and the names each declares;\n- every origin the pages load from;\n- everything written to the reader's machine, and where;\n- every `env` name, and whether it is public;\n- the policy this build ships;\n- what the compiler itself depends on.\n\nAdd `--json` to get the same report for a tool. `wf audit` only reports; it never fails a build.")

        Heading("Before you deploy", id: "before-you-deploy").h2

        Markdown("- Run `wf audit`, and read all of it.\n- Set `\"csp\": true`, and make sure the build passes its own check.\n- Check that `_headers` reaches your host, or set the same headers another way.\n- Serve over HTTPS, with HSTS.\n- Keep the session in an httpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookie — not in `persist`.\n- Read no non-public `env` name in a page (the build refuses one).\n- Make sure every `Unsafe.Html` is sanitised, or provably your own markup.\n- Check authorisation on the server for every request, not with `guard:`.\n- Have the server check uploads: type from the bytes, a size limit, a generated filename, storage nothing serves from.")
    }
}
